    "q-335587": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335587",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$",
      "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.8).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.8).\n$$",
