    "q-335667": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335667",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"40px\" align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=4-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+3xy)=-3+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-16x-y & -x \\\\ 3y & -3+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4-16(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 3(0) & -3+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$",
